<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rahmon Nabiyev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1930">1930</a>–<a href="/years/1993">1993</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rahmon Nabiyev was first secretary of the Communist Party of the Tajik SSR from 1982 to 1985. Returning to power in 1991, he was elected the first president of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>, but rising conflict between his supporters and an Islamist-democratic opposition turned into civil war, and he was forced to resign in September 1992.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rahmon-nabiyev.jpg" alt="Rahmon Nabiyev" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: EgemenMedia · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rahmon_Nabiyev_1991-1992_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rahmon Nabiyevich Nabiyev was born on 5 October 1930 into a family of ordinary farmers of Tajik ethnicity in the Khojent District of the Leninabad Oblast. At 16 he began work as an accountant on a collective farm and entered the Leninabad Agricultural College, from which he graduated in 1949. He then studied at the Tashkent Institute of Irrigation and Agricultural Mechanization Engineers, and after graduating in 1954 he worked for two years as chief engineer of the machine-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tractor">tractor</a> station in Isfisor. He joined the Communist Party of the Tajik SSR in 1961 and became the republic's Minister of Agriculture in 1971 and Chairman of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-european-union">Council of Ministers</a> in 1973.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1982 Nabiyev became First Secretary of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>, which made him head of the republic. He was dismissed in 1985 &quot;for addiction to revelry and alcohol&quot;. From 1986 to 1991 he chaired the Tajik SSR's Nature Conservation Society, and on 23 September 1991 he became chairman of the Supreme Soviet, and so president. He stepped down 13 days later under pressure, then won the presidential election of November 1991 amid accusations of fraud by his opponents and was sworn in on 2 December. On 21 December 1991 he signed the Alma-Ata Protocol founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-independent-states">Commonwealth of Independent States</a>, and in May 1992 the Collective Security Treaty.</p><p>Protests against his government began in March 1992 and demanded democratic reforms. After disputes over the election, street demonstrations grew into civil war from 5 May 1992. On 7 September 1992 opposition forces ambushed Nabiyev on his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dushanbe">Dushanbe</a> airport and forced him to resign at gunpoint in the terminal. The Supreme Soviet accepted his resignation on 19 November and abolished the presidency.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Nabiyev died on 11 April 1993. The official cause was a heart attack, but other versions say he shot himself or was killed, and his family, including his daughter Munavvara Nabiyeva, doubted the official account. He was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khujand">Khujand</a> after a state funeral. Streets and schools in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a> bear his name. He is remembered for his part in the early history of independent Tajikistan and criticised for the civil war, which killed up to 150,000 people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rahmon Nabiyev” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ghafurov District, in what is now <a href="/countries/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khujand">Khujand</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.43,69.74&amp;year=1970" aria-label="Ghafurov District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6262 719 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rahmon Nabiyev"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6712 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6712 989h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.43,69.74&amp;year=1970">What was here in 1970? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
